    "description": "Everyday etiquette in Iran includes respectful forms of address, context-sensitive greetings and reciprocal gestures of courtesy. Expectations vary with familiarity and setting, so descriptions of customary behavior are best treated as starting points for understanding an interaction.",
    "tags": ["Etiquette", "Greetings", "Communication", "Taarof", "Everyday life"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:46:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:46:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Everyday Etiquette in Iran: Greetings, Courtesy and Communication",
      "description": "Understand Iranian greetings, respectful address, taarof and household etiquette while allowing for differences in setting and personal preference."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "greetings-and-address",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with recognition and respect",
        "content": "A greeting does more than open a conversation: it acknowledges the other person and the relationship. The Cultural Atlas guide identifies *salam* as a Persian greeting and describes titles as useful markers of respect. *Agha* and *khanoom* are forms of address for men and women; professional titles may also matter in formal introductions. Its guidance favors a title and surname until a more familiar form is invited. [s1]\n\nPhysical greetings require attention to the people involved. The guide documents handshakes, affectionate greetings among acquaintances and a hand placed over the heart with a slight nod. It also describes reservations about contact between men and women. These are contextual patterns, not permission to initiate touch. An appropriate practical inference is to begin with a verbal greeting and allow the other person’s response to clarify what feels comfortable. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "courtesy-in-action",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Courtesy can involve an exchange",
        "content": "*Taarof* names a repertoire of verbal and nonverbal courtesy. Cultural Atlas illustrates it through exchanges over who should go first and through offers that elevate the other person’s comfort above the speaker’s. A courteous offer or refusal can therefore carry relational meaning beyond its literal wording. The guide also notes that this formality relaxes among close friends. [s2]\n\nIt would be misleading to reduce the practice to a fixed number of refusals. The relevant question is what the participants understand the exchange to mean. For a newcomer, a clear, appreciative response can help distinguish a genuine preference from an initial polite hesitation. This is practical editorial guidance, rather than a universal Iranian formula. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "household-cues",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Notice the household’s cues",
        "content": "Small observations can make a visit easier. Cultural Atlas advises following a shoeless host’s example at the entrance and giving particular recognition to elders. It also describes seating arrangements that express hospitality and respect. Where people sit on the floor, stretching one’s legs toward other guests or elders can be considered discourteous. These details concern the arrangement of a particular home; they do not imply that every household uses the same furniture or reception style. [s2]\n\nConversation, too, can depend on context. The companion communication guide describes indirect phrasing, stories and sayings as ways of conveying meaning while avoiding embarrassment. Its account is a broad orientation, rather than a prediction about an individual. Asking a considerate clarifying question is more useful than assuming that an unfamiliar expression has a hidden meaning. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "setting-and-diversity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formality changes with the relationship",
        "content": "The communication guide distinguishes more formal public behavior from interactions among close friends in private. It also notes that diaspora settings can differ. This makes setting essential to interpretation: warmth at home and restraint elsewhere need not be contradictory, and a reserved greeting does not by itself establish hostility. [s3]\n\nEtiquette also extends beyond a single religious community. A research article on Iranian Zoroastrian speech etiquette describes both distinctive expressions and forms shared with Persian. Its English abstract emphasizes repetition, intonation, gestures and exaggerated respect for the interlocutor. Only that abstract was inspected, so it supports this limited observation rather than detailed claims about the article’s examples. [s4]\n\nTaken together, these accounts favor attentive participation over memorizing a national rulebook. Respectful address, room for clarification and sensitivity to personal boundaries provide a useful beginning; the actual interaction supplies the detail. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
